          %h2#Def Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong algebraic lattice
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
              which is
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattice"} complete lattice
              %li
                %p
                  such that every element is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join"} join
                  of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+elements"} compact elements
          %p
            An
            %strong algebraic lattice
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+lattice"} complete lattice
            (equivalently, a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suplattice"} suplattice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+property"} property
            of having arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            but with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+colimits"} directed colimits
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+joins"} directed joins
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supremum"} supremum
            of the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+element"} compact element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            is compact if, for every subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            of the lattice,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            is less than or equal to the supremum of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            just in case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
            is less than or equal to the supremum of some finite subset of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p Here is an alternative formulation:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              An algebraic lattice is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              which is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+finitely+presentable+category"} locally finitely presentable
              as a category.
          %p
            This formulation suggests a useful way of viewing algebraic lattices in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Ulmer+duality"} Gabriel-Ulmer duality
            (but with regard to enrichment in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %p
            As this last formulation suggests, algebraic lattices typically arise as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+lattices"} subobject lattices
            for objects in locally finitely presentable categories. As an example, for any (finitary)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Alg
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alg
            is an algebraic lattice (this class of examples explains the origin of the term “algebraic lattice”, which is due to Garrett Birkhoff). In fact, all algebraic lattices arise this way (see Theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GS"}
            below).
          %p It is trivial that every finite lattice is algebraic.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#the_category_of_algebraic_lattices The category of algebraic lattices
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            most commonly considered between algebraic lattices are the
            %span.newWikiWord
              finitary functors
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/finitary+functors"} ?
            between them, which is to say, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+topology"} Scott-continuous
            functions between them; i.e., those functions which preserve directed joins (hence the parenthetical remarks
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Def"} above
          %p
            The resulting category
            %strong AlgLat
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed"} cartesian closed
            and is dually equivalent to the category whose objects are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meet+semilattices"} meet semilattices
            (construed as categories with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            over
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            between them (using the convention that a
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is a functor
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^{op} \times C \rightarrow V
          %p
            There is a
            %em full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+embedding"} embedding
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi AlgLat
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Top
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon AlgLat \to Top_0
          %p
            to the category of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axioms"} spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            to the space whose points are elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+sets"} open sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            are defined by the property that their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+maps"} characteristic maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_U: L \to \mathbf{2}
          %p
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi χ
                    %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_U(a) = 1
            if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in U
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi χ
                      %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi_U(a) = 0
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+colimits"} directed colimits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/specialization+order"} specialization order
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(L)
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            again.
          %p
            Every
            = succeed "-space" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            occurs as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspace"} subspace
            of some space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(L)
            associated with an algebraic lattice. Explicitly, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(X)
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            of the underlying set of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P{|\mathcal{O}(X)|}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to (i\circ L)(X)
          %p
            to take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(x) \coloneqq \{U \in \mathcal{O}(X): x \in U\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(L(X))
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              On similar grounds, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi AlgLat
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \colon AlgLat \to Set
              is the forgetful functor, then the
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property#a_factorisation_system_14"} 2-image
              of the projection functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \colon Set\downarrow U \to Set
              is the category of topological spaces
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Top
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi L
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S, L, f \colon S \to U(L))
              gives a space with underlying set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              and open sets those of the form
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi O
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}(O)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
              ranges over the Scott topology on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f \colon S \to S', g \colon L \to L')
              is a morphism in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set \downarrow U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is continuous with respect to these topologies. Therefore the projection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \colon Set \downarrow U \to Set
              factors through the faithful forgetful functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Top
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top \to Set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi im
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(\pi) \to Top
              filling in as the diagonal
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Set
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi U
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi Top
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 2
                            %mtext -
                            %mi im
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi π
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi Set
                            %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    Set \downarrow U &amp; \to &amp; Top \\
                    \\downarrow &amp; \nearrow &amp; \downarrow \\
                    2\text{-}im(\pi) &amp; \to &amp; Set.
                    }
            %p
              But notice also that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set \downarrow U \to Top
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/ternary+factorization+system#examples_9"} eso and full
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}(S)
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              can be reconstituted from the triple
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi S
                      %mo →
                      %mi P
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S, P{|\mathcal{O}(S)|}, x \mapsto N(x) \colon S \to P{|\mathcal{O}(S)|})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to X'
              between topological spaces induces a continuous map between their
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
              reflections
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \to X_{0}'
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P{|\mathcal{O}(X)|}
              (being continuous lattices) are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+objects"} injective objects
              in the category of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
              spaces, we are able to complete to a diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi P
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mi 𝒪
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ′
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi X
                              %mn 0
                            %mo ′
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi P
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                              %mi 𝒪
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi X
                              %mo ′
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp; \to &amp; X_0 &amp; \to &amp; P{|\mathcal{O}(X)|} \\
                    \\downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow \\
                    X' &amp; \to &amp; X_{0}' &amp; \to &amp; P{|\mathcal{O}(X')|}
                    }
            %p
              where the rightmost vertical arrow is Scott-continuous (and the horizontal composites are of the form
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \mapsto N(x)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set \downarrow U \to Top
              is eso and full, it follows that
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi im
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} im(\pi) \to Top
              is eso, full, and faithful, and therefore an equivalence of categories.
            %p
              This connection is explored in more depth with the category of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equilogical+spaces"} equilogical spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} partial equivalence relations
              over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi AlgLat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AlgLat
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relations"} equivalence relations
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
              topological spaces.
          %h3#RelationToLocallyFinitelyPresentableCategories Relation to locally finitely presentable categories
          %p
            One of our definitions of algebraic lattice is: a poset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            which is locally finitely presentable when viewed as a category. The completeness of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            means that right adjoints
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \to Set
            are representable, given by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(p, -) \colon L \to Set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimits"} filtered colimits
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            By
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Ulmer+duality"} Gabriel-Ulmer duality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is determined from the join-semilattice of compact elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi Lex
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \cong Lex(K^{op}, Set)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^{op}
            are subterminal, we can also write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi Lex
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \cong Lex(K^{op}, 2)
            where
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi Sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 = Sub(1)
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Porst)
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+finitely+presentable+category"} locally finitely presentable category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is an object of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The lattice of subobjects
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Sub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sub(X)
              %li
                %p
                  the lattice of quotient objects (equivalence classes of epis sourced at
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Quot
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Quot(X)
              %li
                %p
                  the lattice of congruences (internal equivalence relations) on
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p are all algebraic lattices.
          %p
            This is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Porst"} Porst
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the category of algebras of an Lawvere theory, then the lattice of quotient objects of an algebra is isomorphic to its congruence lattice, as such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+category"} exact category
          %h3#congruence_lattices Congruence lattices
          %p The following result is due to Grätzer and Schmidt:
          #GS.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Every algebraic lattice is isomorphic to the congruence lattice of some
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              of some finitary algebraic theory.
          %p
            In particular, since every finite lattice is algebraic, every finite lattice arises this way. Remarkably, it is not known at this time whether every finite lattice arises as the congruence lattice of a
            %em finite
            algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong false
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/196074/2926"} MO discussion
          %p
            Another problem which had long remained open is the congruence lattice problem: is every
            %em distributive
            algebraic lattice the congruence lattice (or lattice of quotient objects) of some lattice
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congruence_lattice_problem"} Wikipedia article
          %h3#completely_distributive_lattices Completely distributive lattices
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandroff+locales"} Alexandroff locales
              is equivalent to that of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+distributive+lattice"} completely distributive
              algebraic lattices.
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#Caramello"}> Caramello, remark 4.3
            ).
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+distributive+lattice"} completely distributive
            algebraic lattices form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            of that of all distributive lattices. The reflector is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+extension"} canonical extension
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+element"} compact element
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                compact element in a locale
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/compact+element+in+a+locale"} ?
